{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789773373,"nonce":"Ur0L2hajr3lIxkH5rGDCQJfcgzI=","title":"Cranelift moved one word for a struct field that is itself a struct","message":"`insertvalue` of an aggregate into a struct field stored a single word\n(the address of the inserted aggregate's stack copy, or its first field),\nand `extractvalue` of such a field loaded a single word and handed it on\nas the aggregate. The HIR types the field; nothing in the backend looked\nat how wide it was.\n\nReproducer: `Outer { tag: i64, inner: Pair }` with `Pair { a, b }`; build\n`o = {7, {3, 40}}`, then rebuild the inserted pair as `{9, 40}` and read\n`o.inner.a`: 9 (an alias of the pair's storage) instead of 3, and\n`o.inner.b` read from uninitialised stack. The interpreter, which holds\nevery multi-field aggregate by address and copies on insert, was right.\n\nFix: a field or array element whose type is held by reference (the same\nrule the interpreter uses, `hir_interp::held`) is read as the address of\nits bytes inside the parent's storage and written by copying its bytes in.\nCovered by `crates/compiler/tests/nested_aggregate_fields.rs` on Cranelift,\nthe interpreter and LLVM.\n\nThis is the first prerequisite of shaped tuples in the Python frontend:\n`Type::Tuple` lowers to an anonymous struct, and nested tuples (a tuple of\ntuples, a tuple holding a list header) are nested struct fields.","files":null}]}